---
import CourseLayout from "../../layouts/CourseLayout.astro";
import Outcomes from "../../components/Outcomes.astro";
import SourceTable from "../../components/SourceTable.astro";
import { evidenceUrl } from "../../lib/evidence";
import { markdownTable } from "../../lib/mdtable";

const terms = markdownTable("TERMINOLOGY.md");
---

<CourseLayout
  title="Glossary"
  kicker="Reference"
  description="Course shorthand, with the qualification attached. Several of these
    definitions exist mainly to stop a word from meaning two things in one sentence."
>
  <p>
    Read the second half of each definition. Most of the trouble in this field
    is not in the concepts but in the places where a term quietly changes scope
    — a basic block that one tool splits at calls and another does not, an
    UNSAT that holds under a path prefix and is then quoted as an invariant.
  </p>

  <h2>Four outcomes, before anything else</h2>
  <p>
    These four are the course's working vocabulary. They appear on every week
    page, in every ledger, and in all three assessment rubrics, and almost
    every wrong conclusion in this field is one of them being read as another.
  </p>
  <Outcomes />

  <h2>Terms</h2>
  <p>
    The {terms.rows.length} definitions below are the research package's own, rendered
    from{" "}
    <a href={evidenceUrl("TERMINOLOGY.md")}>TERMINOLOGY.md</a> while the site
    builds.
  </p>
  <SourceTable table={terms} class="sc-wide" />

  <h2>Two notes on numbers</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Width.</strong> Most arithmetic claims in this corpus concern
    <code>uint32</code> values modulo 2<sup>32</sup>. An identity checked over the
    integers is not the same claim, and the difference is not academic —
    wraparound is where several of these transformations hide.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Convention.</strong> Two different control-flow-graph conventions
    appear in this course's evidence. The dedicated PE-byte audit used for the
    branch-rewriting study does not split blocks at calls and treats
    <code>ret</code> as terminal. The Miasm recursive graphs used for the
    unflattening study do split at calls. Counts from the two are not
    interchangeable, and every page that quotes one says which.
  </p>
</CourseLayout>
